'''anesthetize, anæsthetize''' (an-es′thē̤-tīz), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''anesthetized'', ''anæsthetized'', ppr. ''anesthetizing'', ''anæsthetizing''. [< ''anesthetic'' + ''-ize''.] To bring under the influence of an anesthetic agent, as chloroform, a freezing-mixture, etc.; render insensible, especially to pain. Also spelled ''anesthetise'' and ''anæsthetise''.

'''anet†''' (an′et), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''annet'', ''ennet'', < ME. ''anete'', < OF. ''anet'', also ''aneth'', < L. ''anethum'', < Gr. ἄνηθον, later Attic ἄνισον, anise, dill: see ''anise''.] The common dill, ''Carum'' (or ''Anethum'') ''graveolens''.

'''anethene''' (an′e-thēn), ''n.'' [< L. ''anethum'', anise (see ''anet''), + ''-ene''.] The most volatile part (C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>16</sub>) of the essence of oil of dill.

'''anethol''' (an′e-thol), ''n.'' [< L. ''anethum'', anise (see ''anet''), + ''-ol''.] The chief constituent (C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O) of the essential oils of anise and fennel. {{smaller|It exists in two forms, one a solid at ordinary temperature (anise-camphor or solid anethol), the other a liquid (liquid anethol).}}

